      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "A prospective clinical analysis of headaches in whiplash injury."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To provide a detailed clinical account of the nature, characteristics and natural history of headaches, and to examine their possible relation to the neck injury."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "One medicolegal practice in UK."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This study consists of a prospective clinical analysis of headache symptoms in consecutive patients referred to the author for medicolegal assessment of whiplash injury, with no special reference to headaches."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "All 80 patients noted neck pain. Headaches were related as a consequence of the accident by 48 patients (60% of the total). The circumstances of injury did not differ from whiplash victims who did not have prominent headache. The headache onset was maximum in the first 24 h after injury. The failure to recall well documented pre-accident headaches in almost one fifth of patients is significant. The common types of headaches were non-specific, generalised, dull, aching pain (25 patients), a mixtures of aching and tightness, and tension type headache. Only three (6%) had migraine without aura."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Post-whiplash headache is a genuine common but short lived affair, constant headaches disappearing within 3 weeks in 85% subjects. In the minority complaining of headache after that period there was no evidence of persisting physical injury, nor of inability to work or other disability caused by headaches."
        }
      ]},
